  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a corresponding picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191822/assets/b1d04d90855560e36762d492f34ac14fb960c98f55c234da7c706e638fd9ea9e.png)\n\n   \n\nLet $z$ be the measure of the exterior angle at $C$ pictured above. Since the sum of the exterior angles of a triangle is ${360}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{100}^{∘}+{122}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {222}^{∘}+z & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={138}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the value of the missing interior angle at $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & ={180}^{∘}-z \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{138}^{∘} \\\\  & ={42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠C={42}^{∘}.$",
